WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.520
A month after Germany's general election, the&nbsp;
country may not yet have a new government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.440
but the newly elected Parliament will take up&nbsp;
work on Tuesday. The Bundestag will be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.960
diverse than ever, with younger MPs, trans MPs&nbsp;
and the first ever Black woman to be elected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.000
to the German Parliament. DW met up with&nbsp;
a woman who hopes to be the first of many.&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.840
There's a lot to do - and no time to lose. Awet&nbsp;
Tesfaiesus has been working around the clock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.000
since the general election that secured&nbsp;
her a spot in Germany’s history books.&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:43.200
The Eritrean-born lawyer had been involved in&nbsp;
local politics for years. Then a racist killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:50.080
spree in her home state shook her to the core.
"I don’t know why, but the terror attack in Hanau&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.880
pushed me over the edge. I thought something&nbsp;
has to change. I can’t stand this any longer."&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.000
10 people were killed in the terror attack - 9&nbsp;
of them first and second generation immigrants.&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.760
Overcome by the feeling that Germany was no&nbsp;
longer a safe place for her and her family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:09.280
she considered leaving the country.&nbsp;

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:16.960
"But frankly: it's hard to find&nbsp;
a country that's free of racism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.080
So the next option was staying&nbsp;
and doing everything possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:32.240
to fight against discrimination and racism."
Here, in a rural area of the state of Hesse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.320
a woman of color on the ballot is a novelty. But&nbsp;
the lack of footsteps to follow was all the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:44.880
encouragement for the 47-year-old. To her it's&nbsp;
very clear: You can't be what you can’t see.&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.600
"When a third or a quarter of our&nbsp;
schoolchildren have an immigration background,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.960
why are we not seeing that proportion among&nbsp;
teachers? We should! As a schoolgirl, I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.520
to see myself reflected in my teachers. Same&nbsp;
goes for the authorities and our parliaments."&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.880
But as German society grows more and more diverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.720
statistics reveal that institutions&nbsp;
are only catching up very slowly.&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.920
The Bundestag has 736 members.
83 of them have roots in a different country.&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.840
26% of German citizens have&nbsp;
an immigration background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:28.800
In the Bundestag, it’s only around 11%.
Less than HALF of these MPs are women.&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.200
And until Tesfaiesus was elected, there&nbsp;
hadn’t been a single Black voice among them.&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.280
From her small-town constituency&nbsp;
straight into Berlin’s center of power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.120
After her election success,&nbsp;
she still has to pinch herself.&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:52.400
"I still haven’t completely processed&nbsp;
it. It's a tremendous honor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.280
This is not just about me. This is an&nbsp;
historic moment for all of us, for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:09.200
entire Black community. This is a huge deal."
When asked why it took until 2021 for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.640
Black woman to get a seat in the&nbsp;
Bundestag, she doesn’t hesitate.&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.840
"Because there's structural racism in our country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.440
And if we say, it doesn't matter that I'm&nbsp;
Black, we're ignoring these structural problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:28.720
That means we're not ready to acknowledge that -&nbsp;
as a Black woman – I'm presented with different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:34.400
circumstances and obstacles than a woman who&nbsp;
is not Black. No matter how qualified I am."&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.480
For now, Tesfaiesus only comes to&nbsp;
the Bundestag to pick up her mail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.200
But she can’t wait to hit the ground running.&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.000
"I hope to be able to focus more attention&nbsp;
on the issue of equal opportunities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:54.640
We need to create awareness&nbsp;
but also push for legislation."&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.400
The new MP has many plans.&nbsp;
But the biggest of them all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:13.840
is to serve to others as the&nbsp;
role model she never had.

